Ross‑OS Licensing Model
The ROSS‑OS Constitutional Substrate uses MIT because:

it preserves architectural openness

it allows broad adoption

it supports commercial and non‑commercial use

it avoids restrictive copyleft entanglements

it aligns with the substrate’s deterministic, modular design

Higher‑level Ross‑OS components (e.g., CopWan Ledger Physics, Trinity Prism, Everlight 24) may use additional licensing layers, but the substrate itself remains MIT to ensure maximum interoperability.

For guidance on multi‑layer licensing across the Ross‑OS ecosystem, see:
Ross‑OS Licensing Strategy

